The build gate and INC-007

This section exists because one number in Table 1 was wrong, and the way it became wrong says more about the record than the error itself. In one revision, the wall-clock total of Table 1 was printed as 860. The four per-wave maxima printed with it are 43, 109, 525 and 163 seconds, which sum to 840. The 860 did not come from the instrument or from the files. It began as an arithmetic error in a reviewer's text, was carried into the review chair's required-change list, and was then copied into the table by the author without being recomputed. Each pair of hands the number passed through assumed the previous pair had done the arithmetic. The incident is recorded in the incident log as INC-007.

For a reader who wants to reproduce the record, the shape of this failure matters more than its size. Nothing about the 860 was hidden. The four maxima sit in the same table, a few lines above the total, and summing them takes seconds. The error survived because the review chain conferred authority: once the chair's required-change list contained the number, it read as a requirement rather than as a claim, and a requirement is not something an author is expected to re-derive. The lesson the project recorded is blunt, and it is recorded in the incident log rather than left implicit: a reviewer's number is a provenance lead, not provenance. It points at where to check. It is not itself the check.

The structural fix is a build gate. The PDF build script now runs scripts/check_table1.sh before any document is produced. The script recomputes every instrument-derived cell of Table 1 from the instrument table itself: the per-wave byte counts, the four wall-clock maxima, the 840 total, and the resolver row. If any recomputed value disagrees with what the table claims, the build fails and no PDF is emitted. The script also pins the locale, so number formatting is stable, and a comparison cannot fail or pass spuriously because of thousands separators or decimal marks on a different machine. For a reproducer this changes what the printed table is. Table 1 is no longer a static claim that happens to match the files; it is a projection of the files that the build re-derives and would refuse to print otherwise. If you rebuild the PDF yourself, the same gate runs on your side, and any drift you might have caught by hand is caught before the document exists.

The boundary of the gate is as important as the gate, because the gate is deliberately narrow. It covers instrument-derived cells only. Three cells of Table 1 are hub-audited rather than instrument-derived: the dispatch count N, the admitted characters, and the admitted fraction. These cannot be recomputed by any reader, because they come from the hub's private store. The uncomfortable fact, recorded alongside the gate rather than smoothed over, is that these are the cells that have failed most often. The gate is therefore strongest exactly where reproduction was already easy, and silent exactly where the record has historically been weakest. For the hub-audited cells a reproducer depends on the hub's audit trail and on the accuracy of the transcription between that trail and the table; the build gate does nothing for them. A reader who wants independent assurance on the dispatch count, the admitted characters, or the admitted fraction cannot get it from a rebuild, and this section will not pretend otherwise.

The incident log also records that the lesson was not learned in a single move. Two later addenda record four more reviewer-supplied sentences transcribed into the record without recomputation, among them a per-brief range stated over twelve briefs that was in fact true of ten. None of these were caught at transcription time, for the same reason the 860 survived: the sentences arrived inside review text and were copied as findings rather than treated as leads. Each was caught later by someone returning to the files and counting again. The pattern is worth naming plainly for a reproducer: transcription without recomputation has occurred repeatedly in this record, and the honest response is not assurance that it will not happen again but a rule that keeps every such sentence checkable after the fact. Treat a reviewer-supplied sentence as a pointer to a recount. The recount is the fact; the sentence is only where the recount was suggested.

A second, adjacent failure mode is recorded as an addendum to INC-003. A later revision log entry claimed a dispatcher-header sentence that the committed file did not contain. The claim was not caught by the build gate, because it was neither a number nor part of Table 1; it was caught by three reviewers who opened the file. The diagnosis matters more than the slip. The entry had asserted a post-state from the fix list, not from the diff: the author had written down what the change was supposed to produce instead of what the change had produced. The rule adopted from that addendum is that every revision-log line is checked against the diff before the tag. For a reproducer, this defines the trust boundary of the revision log itself. The log is a series of claims about states of the repository, and before the rule it was possible for a line to describe a state nobody had verified. Reading the log is therefore not the same as reading evidence. It is reading assertions that are supposed to have been checked, each of which can be re-checked by opening the file the line points at.

Read together, the gate and the two incident threads give a reproducer a precise map of what this record guarantees and what it does not. Where a Table 1 cell is instrument-derived, the build guarantees it matches the instrument table, and you can verify that guarantee by running the build, or by recomputing the cell by hand; the class of error behind the 860 cannot recur there silently. Where a cell is hub-audited, the build guarantees nothing about it, because it comes from the hub's private store and cannot be recomputed by any reader. Where a sentence is reviewer-supplied, the standing rule is that the author re-derives it from the files every time, and the addenda exist because that rule was written down after it had been violated. Where a revision-log line describes a state, the rule is that the line was checked against the diff before the tag, which makes the log checkable line by line rather than trustworthy as a whole. That division is what INC-007 contributes to the argument of the paper: an institution, agentic or otherwise, earns trust not by relying on honest participants but by making specific failures recomputable, specific claims checkable against their sources, and the limits of both stated in public.

AB-S5-OK
